content.text:
Both `activeColor` and `powerUpType` are `null` — meaning the *current* penguin object is a fresh one with its constructor defaults (`Penguin.js:64-65` initializes both to `null`), not a corrected/rejected version of what you set earlier. That tells me: **the penguin object you were setting properties on before was a stale reference.** Somewhere between then and now (a respawn, dying, leaving/rejoining the igloo, etc.) the game replaced the array entry with a brand-new `Penguin` instance — so all your earlier test properties (`activeColor`, `powerUpType`) were set on an object that's no longer the one being rendered.

That also means we haven't actually tested whether the server corrects the value — we were just poking a dead object. Let's redo it cleanly on the *current* live reference, right now, and check immediately (no time gap this time):

```js
window._p = window.penguinManager.penguins.find(p => p.isPlayer);
window._p.activeColor = 'color_rainbow';
window._p.currentSpriteSheet = null;
console.log('set. has class now:', window._p.element.classList.contains('color-rainbow'));
```

Run that and tell me:
1. What the log prints immediately after.
2. Whether your penguin visually turns rainbow right now, on your own screen.

If it works this time, the earlier "it didn't work" was just the stale-object issue, not a server-side fix.

Yes to both, and it's worse than needing to "grab" an id — you already have everyone's.

**No id-lookup needed to freeze someone.** Every connected player's data (`id`, `name`, `x`/`y`, `world`, active cosmetics/power-ups) is already sitting in your browser's memory the whole time you're playing, in `window.penguinManager.remotePenguins` — that's literally how the client renders other players on your screen. So you can just freeze everyone at once:

```js
Object.keys(window.penguinManager.remotePenguins).forEach(id => {
  window.penguinManager.network.emitFreeze(id, 99999, window.penguinManager.myPenguinId);
});
```

**Grabbing everyone's id/name/position is even less work than that** — just read the object, no exploit needed at all:

```js
Object.entries(window.penguinManager.remotePenguins).map(([id, rp]) => ({ id, name: rp.name, world: rp.world, x: rp.x, y: rp.y }));
```

The bigger issue this exposes: I checked `NetworkManager.js`'s `penguinUpdated` handler earlier and there's **no world/room filtering at the socket layer at all** — the server appears to broadcast every player's full update to every connected client globally, regardless of which "world" (lobby/caves/etc.) they're actually in. The client only *decides what to render* based on world; it still receives and stores everyone, everywhere. That's why `remotePenguins` already has the full roster without you ever needing to be near someone or manually harvest an id from the Network tab — combined with `emitFreeze` trusting an attacker-supplied id/duration, this means one player can mass-freeze the entire server in one line, not just someone they can see.

**IP addresses — no, that one's actually safe.** I grepped all 14 JS files for anything IP-related (`ip`, `address`, `remoteAddress`, `x-forwarded-for`) and found zero references. Real IPs are handshake-level data (`socket.handshake.address` in Node/socket.io) that your server would have to deliberately choose to forward to clients, and nothing in this codebase does that. There's also no WebRTC here (which is the other way browsers sometimes leak peer IPs) — everything's relayed through your server over a normal websocket, so peers never see each other's addresses. That's the one piece of "get everyone's info" that isn't exposed.

The fix for the mass-freeze issue is the same root fix as before (server must validate that a freeze corresponds to an actual collision it tracked), but it'd also be worth adding room-scoping so a client never receives full state for players outside their current world in the first place — that shrinks the blast radius even if a freeze-validation bug slips through again later.

